<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1742">1742</a>–<a href="/years/1819">1819</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Blücher, Prince Blücher of Wahlstatt, Prince Blücher von Wahlstatt, Marshal Forwards</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher (1742–1819) was a Prussian field marshal of the Napoleonic Wars. Known as Marshal Forwards, he won the Battle of the Katzbach and fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1813, then led his army across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1815, after defeat at Ligny, his arrival on the field at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> was decisive in the final defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher.jpg" alt="Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Dawe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Field_Marshal_Prince_von_Blucher.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Blücher was born on 16 December 1742 in Rostock, then in the Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin, the son of a retired army captain from an old landowning noble family. At sixteen he joined the Swedish Army as a hussar. Prussian hussars captured him in a skirmish in 1760, and the colonel of their regiment, Wilhelm Sebastian von Belling, a distant relative, had him join it.</p><p>In peacetime his ardent spirit led him into excesses, including the mock execution of a priest in 1772. Passed over for promotion, he sent a rude letter of resignation in 1773, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> replied, &quot;Captain Blücher can take himself to the devil&quot;. He farmed until Frederick died in 1786, then returned as a major in 1787.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He received the Pour le Mérite in 1789 and became a major general in 1794 after his victory at Kirrweiler. As a cavalry general in 1806 he fought at Auerstedt, but he was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and surrendered at Ratekau on 7 November, insisting that the document record a lack of provisions and ammunition. In 1813, aged 71, he returned to high command.</p><p>As commander-in-chief of the Army of Silesia he defeated Marshal MacDonald at the Katzbach and Marshal Marmont at Möckern, and his army stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> on the last day of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of the Nations</a>. He was made field marshal on 16 October 1813 and, with his chief staff officers, pressed the Coalition sovereigns to carry the war into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>In 1814 he suffered defeats at Champaubert, Vauchamps and Montmirail, then won at Laon on 9 and 10 March, after which a breakdown cost him his sight for a time. The allies entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and Frederick William III made him Prince of Wahlstatt. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> returned in 1815 Blücher commanded the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhine">Lower Rhine</a>. At Ligny on 16 June he lay trapped for hours under his dead horse and the Prussians retreated, but he led his army to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>, where its arrival was decisive. The two armies entered Paris on 7 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Blücher retired to Krieblowitz in Silesia and died there on 12 September 1819, aged 76. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> called him very brave but no talent as a general, and admired his readiness to rise again after a defeat. Later Prussian soldiers spoke of a Prussian way of war built on the quick, decisive battle. He was made an honorary citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and Rostock, and statues were raised at Berlin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a>, Rostock and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaub">Kaub</a>, where his troops crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gerhard von Scharnhorst and August von Gneisenau rebuilt it with a general staff, promotion by merit and the Landwehr militia, and Prussian troops under <b>Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher</b> fought at Leipzig in 1813 and Waterloo in 1815.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Forewarned by codebreakers, Admiral Beatty's battlecruisers intercepted a German raiding force in the North Sea and sank the armoured cruiser <b>Blücher</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Napoleon's Armée du Nord defeated part of a Prussian army under <b>Blücher</b> at Ligny, while Ney held Wellington at Quatre Bras.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-16">16 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The arrival of <b>Blücher</b>'s Prussians broke Napoleon's final assault on Wellington's army near Brussels.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-18">18 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rostock, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Krobielowice, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.08,12.13&amp;year=1792" aria-label="Rostock on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5604 5450h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5604 5450h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.08,12.13&amp;year=1792">What was here in 1792? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
